#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087498 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087828 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbe35c7b-8068-5b28-a820-d25e6b62565a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With Complex Roots Using the Quadratic Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "2e80a8b3-de45-51f0-9241-a699222da37b" :question/math-academy-id "e-4157" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\, x^2-4x+5=0$." :question/worked-solution "We can find the roots of a quadratic equation using the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\nx = \\frac{-b\\pm\\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the coefficients are $a=1, b=-4$, and $c=5$. Substituting these values into the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= (- (-4) ± \\sqrt{(-4)^{2} - 4(1)(5)})/(2(1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 ± \\sqrt{16 - 20}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 ± \\sqrt{-4}}{2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 ± 2\\textrm{i}}{2} \\\\\n&= 2 ± \\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions,\n\n$$\nx=2+\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx=2-\\textrm{i}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087499 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087830 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9c8c1d8-2662-5c15-b084-084f9e930141" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Equation With Complex Roots by Completing the Square"}] :question/id #uuid "af6ecee9-30de-5913-be21-f184d6f9cacd" :question/math-academy-id "e-4160" :question/problem "The equation $x^2+8x+80=0$ can be written in the form $(x+p)^2=q$. Find $p+q$, and solve the equation." :question/worked-solution "We first move the constant to the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 8x + 80 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} + 8x &= -80\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we complete the square on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 8x &= -80 \\\\\nx^{2} + 2 \\cdot 4x + 4^{2} - 4^{2} &= -80 \\\\\n(x + 4)^{2} - 16 &= -80 \\\\\n(x + 4)^{2} &= -64\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=4$, $q=-64$, and\n\n$$\np+q = -60\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve the equation using the square root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(x + 4)^{2} &= -64 \\\\\nx + 4 &= ± \\sqrt{-64} \\\\\nx + 4 &= ± 8\\textrm{i} \\\\\nx &= -4 ± 8\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions,\n\n$$\nx=-4+8\\,\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx=-4-8\\,\\textrm{i}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087632 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087829 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef8dec7d-5154-59ee-aee8-9f068eb6b8f4" :knowledge-point/title "Rearranging and Solving a Quadratic Equation With Complex Roots"}] :question/id #uuid "7f7e9924-1c7b-516c-928c-962a4cd5cbcc" :question/math-academy-id "e-7517" :question/problem "Find the solutions of the equation $4x^2= 4x-13$." :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation in standard form by bringing all of the terms to the left-hand side. This gives the equation\n\n$$\n4x^2 - 4x + 13=0\n$$\n\nWe can find the roots of a quadratic equation using the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\nx = \\frac{-b\\pm\\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the coefficients are $a=4, b=-4$, and $c=13$. Substituting these values into the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= (- (-4) ± \\sqrt{(-4)^{2} - 4(4)(13)})/(2(4)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 ± \\sqrt{16 - 208}}{8} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 ± \\sqrt{-192}}{8} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 ± 8\\sqrt{-3}}{8} \\\\\n&= \\frac{4 ± 8\\sqrt{3}\\textrm{i}}{8} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} ± \\sqrt{3}\\textrm{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions,\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{1}{2}+ \\sqrt{3} \\, \\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{1}{2}- \\sqrt{3} \\, \\textrm{i}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233676 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087829 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef8dec7d-5154-59ee-aee8-9f068eb6b8f4" :knowledge-point/title "Rearranging and Solving a Quadratic Equation With Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bdb7b3f-b4e5-511c-a044-8968678b8052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232361}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2bdb7b3f-b4e5-511c-a044-8968678b8052" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232361} :answer-field/id #uuid "e898c7ec-99b4-56ec-91de-29be2252ba98" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232360} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0836784b-9a67-52bb-b67c-5452ba0317b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232363}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0836784b-9a67-52bb-b67c-5452ba0317b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232363} :answer-field/id #uuid "04b5d2dc-1588-56be-bf8e-3aeebd68c7d2" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186232362}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "118176bc-6145-5e92-99d8-81ec478422c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-250562" :question/problem "Solve the equation ${x}^{2}+22(x+1)=-100.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\pm $\n{{field-2}}$\\text{i}$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation in standard form by bringing all of the terms to the left-hand side. This gives the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+22(x+1) & =-100 \\\\ {x}^{2}+22x+22+100 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+22x+122 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the roots of a quadratic equation using the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the coefficients are $a=1,\\,b=22,$ and $c=122.$ Substituting these values into the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-22\\pm \\sqrt{(22)^{2}-4(1)(122)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-22\\pm \\sqrt{484-488}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-22\\pm \\sqrt{-4}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-22\\pm 2\\,\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =-11\\pm 1\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=-11+\\text{i}$ and $x=-11-\\text{i}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233738 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087829 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef8dec7d-5154-59ee-aee8-9f068eb6b8f4" :knowledge-point/title "Rearranging and Solving a Quadratic Equation With Complex Roots"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8883de5f-4ad3-53e1-a8c2-678c96063be9" :question/math-academy-id "q-28178" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\,-6x=-{x}^{2}-13.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation in standard form by bringing all of the terms to the left-hand side. This gives the equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-6x+13=0.\n$$\n\nWe can find the roots of a quadratic equation using the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the coefficients are $a=1,\\,b=-6,$ and $c=13.$ Substituting these values into the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{-(-6)\\pm \\sqrt{(-6)^{2}-4(1)(13)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pm \\sqrt{36-52}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pm \\sqrt{-16}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{6\\pm 4\\,\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =3\\pm 2\\,\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has two solutions, $x=3+2\\,\\text{i}$ and $x=3-2\\,\\text{i}.$"}]}
